Cell[TextData[{
 "Out of curiosity, I want to check something: How different is ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["H", "inf"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "a9e3aca8-abd1-49ea-b39f-9aa77a078dfb"],
 " from",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SqrtBox[
    RowBox[{"  ", 
     FormBox[
      FractionBox[
       SubscriptBox["V", "inf"], "3"],
      TraditionalForm]}]], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "afe88579-6d14-4a7f-b8a4-305d7cfd7378"],
 ". This is done later in the notebook. "
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9642810462267447`*^9, 3.964281129525217*^9}, {
  3.964716314306878*^9, 
  3.964716319458269*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"477391a6-6cce-4f0b-9af8-\
b2aa7fc13810"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "Test 2: The Mid-Flow Interpolation Method uses the directly calculated H(\
\[Phi]) from our imports to build a spline for \[CurlyEpsilon] (\[Phi])  =  \
",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CurlyEpsilon]", "dyn"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "ec5e27d5-7c56-4a88-9919-15c7d83e95dc"]
}], "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.964277852704522*^9, 3.964277933533718*^9}, {
  3.964277985329771*^9, 
  3.9642780701168747`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"f8c39c37-3d8f-4696-a08e-\
443d2579f76e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Now, one comparison we can make is the comparison between different methods \
of acquiring \[CurlyEpsilon](\[Phi]) where we use the more dynamical \
\[CurlyEpsilon] with the mask applied to it from our data. In other words ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CurlyEpsilon]", "sr"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "83a259e6-c69d-4626-b85b-5e07b55a4a2a"],
 " (symbolic) is fully from the definition of the potential that has been \
interpolated and plugged into ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["H", "sr"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7bc57707-9d6c-412d-ae6a-aced0e931009"],
 " which we approximate to be the square root of that interpolated potential \
(V)/3. Derivatives of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["H", "sr"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "7373c846-fb33-4445-bdee-50feb5109556"],
 " are used to find the this symbolic ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SubscriptBox["\[CurlyEpsilon]", "sr"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "51e0c2be-9fa0-42d0-9d60-fcd782b4f25f"],
 ". This compares to the original \[CurlyEpsilon] which I will call the \
dynamic form where we make an interpolated function of H from the data and \
then apply an inflationary mask cut off that obeys \[CurlyEpsilon] < 1 . We\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll discuss the third method which will explicitly use flow \
equations in the Test 3 section. \n\nI can also fully use the",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{" ", 
    SubscriptBox["\[CurlyEpsilon]", "dyn"]}], TraditionalForm]],
  ExpressionUUID->"bfade89a-d440-4d0a-bc17-1f261de08d42"],
 " I have here to find all of the slow roll parameters as a function of N. \
Initially, the method I used to find the SRPs required me to define \[Phi](N) \
which I then plugged into the expressions below. Note, the first three \
parameters will use interpolation methods made from the data ."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.964185348068905*^9, 3.964185359736683*^9}, {
  3.96418543705962*^9, 3.96418573806317*^9}, {3.964276864226832*^9, 
  3.964276913355651*^9}, {3.9642781092784967`*^9, 3.96427811482587*^9}, {
  3.964278156966187*^9, 3.9642782142700243`*^9}, {3.96427825108848*^9, 
  3.964278277758295*^9}, {3.964278355165066*^9, 3.964278390214707*^9}, {
  3.9647163598685503`*^9, 
  3.964716371877246*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"99311ab6-909c-47aa-ba52-\
e650c637671f"],

